{"bill":{"id":"119-hr-1722","congress":119,"billType":"hr","billNumber":"1722","title":"Billion Dollar Boondoggle Act","introducedDate":"2025-02-27","latestActionDate":"2026-07-23","latestActionText":"Received in the Senate. Read twice. Placed on Senate Legislative Calendar under General Orders. Calendar No. 467.","policyArea":"Government Operations and Politics","subjects":["Congressional oversight","Government information and archives","Public contracts and procurement"],"sponsorBioguideId":"M001215","cosponsorBioguideIds":["A000381","B001317","F000480"],"committeeCodes":[],"committeeNames":[],"officialSummary":"Billion Dollar Boondoggle Act of 2025 This bill requires the Office of Management and Budget (OMB) to collect information from federal agencies and report to Congress regarding projects that are behind schedule or have expenditures that have exceeded the original cost estimate. Specifically, the bill requires OMB to issue guidance directing federal agencies to annually submit specified information to OMB regarding certain federally funded projects that (1) are more than five years behind schedule, or (2) have expenditures that are at least $1 billion more than the original cost estimate for the project. Among other information, the agencies must submit to OMB a description of each project; an explanation of any change to the original scope of the project; the original and current expected dates for the completion of the project; the original and current cost estimates adjusted for inflation; an explanation for any delays in completing the project or increases in the cost; and the amount of and rationale for any award, incentive fee, or other type of bonus awarded for the project. The bill also requires OMB to submit an annual report to Congress containing the information submitted by the agencies and post the report on the OMB website.","congressDotGovUrl":"https://www.congress.gov/bill/119th-congress/house-bill/1722","source":"congress","sourceUrl":"https://www.govinfo.gov/bulkdata/BILLSTATUS/119/hr/BILLSTATUS-119hr1722.xml","fetchedAt":"2026-07-29T01:29:50.597Z"},"classification":{"billId":"119-hr-1722","plainSummary":"This bill requires the Office of Management and Budget to collect and publish an annual report on federal projects that are either more than five years behind schedule or have gone at least $1 billion over their original cost estimate. Agencies must explain the reasons for delays and cost overruns, describe changes to project scope, and disclose any bonuses or incentive fees paid. The report must be submitted to Congress and posted online.","industries":[{"industry":"defense","confidence":0.85,"rationale":"Defense contractors frequently work on large federal projects that may trigger the reporting thresholds for schedule delays or cost overruns."},{"industry":"construction","confidence":0.75,"rationale":"Construction and engineering firms execute major federal infrastructure and building projects that could exceed cost estimates or timelines."},{"industry":"tech","confidence":0.65,"rationale":"Technology contractors working on large federal IT modernization and systems projects may face increased reporting obligations if projects run over budget or schedule."}],"method":"llm","model":"claude-sonnet-4-5","inputHash":"984f213aa841cfb3c7bffb0b","classifiedAt":"2026-07-30T02:21:23.539Z"},"plain":{"whatItDoes":"This bill would require the Office of Management and Budget to collect information from federal agencies and report to Congress regarding projects that are behind schedule or have expenditures that have exceeded the original cost estimate.","whoItTouches":"Anyone who deals with a federal agency.","everydayEffect":"If it passed, the change would show up in dealing with the government. Nothing has changed yet. Most bills never become law.","confidence":"crs-summary","source":"Reworded from the official summary. Staff at the Congressional Research Service wrote that summary. It is public domain, and t